[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Factory Method vs. Abstract Factory: Design Patterns Tutorial"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Creational design patterns deal with object creation mechanisms, trying to create objects in a manner suitable to the situation. Among these, the Factory Method and Abstract Factory patterns are often confused. This tutorial clarifies their differences, use cases, and implementations."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. Understanding Factory Method"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Factory Method pattern defines an interface for creating an object, but lets subclasses decide which class to instantiate. Factory Method lets a class defer instantiation to subclasses."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Factory Method Example (C# / Java Style)"},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"interface IButton {
    void Render();
}

class WindowsButton : IButton {
    public void Render() { Console.WriteLine("Rendering Windows Button"); }
}

class MacButton : IButton {
    public void Render() { Console.WriteLine("Rendering Mac Button"); }
}

abstract class Dialog {
    public abstract IButton CreateButton();

    public void RenderWindow() {
        IButton okButton = CreateButton();
        okButton.Render();
    }
}

class WindowsDialog : Dialog {
    public override IButton CreateButton() { return new WindowsButton(); }
}"},{"order":7,"type":"heading","text":"2. Understanding Abstract Factory"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Abstract Factory pattern provides an interface for creating families of related or dependent objects without specifying their concrete classes. It is essentially a factory of factories."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Abstract Factory Example"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"interface IGUIFactory {
    IButton CreateButton();
    ICheckbox CreateCheckbox();
}

class WindowsFactory : IGUIFactory {
    public IButton CreateButton() { return new WindowsButton(); }
    public ICheckbox CreateCheckbox() { return new WindowsCheckbox(); }
}

class MacFactory : IGUIFactory {
    public IButton CreateButton() { return new MacButton(); }
    public ICheckbox CreateCheckbox() { return new MacCheckbox(); }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"heading","text":"3. Key Differences"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Focus: Factory Method focuses on a single product type through inheritance. Abstract Factory focuses on multiple, related product families through composition."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Mechanism: Factory Method uses inheritance and overrides a method in a subclass. Abstract Factory uses object composition and delegates creation to a separate factory object."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Complexity: Factory Method is simpler and deals with one creation method. Abstract Factory involves multiple factory methods grouped into a single interface."},{"order":15,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Use Factory Method when you need to provide hooks for subclasses to extend your classes. Use Abstract Factory when your system needs to be independent of how its products are created, composed, and represented, especially when dealing with families of matching objects."}]
